+# the panel's record
+
+Two moments in a show's life leave nothing behind but what the panel itself
+recorded. The dry run, where the table is run into a field of nothing and
+somebody watches lights. And the morning after, when the field has to be
+walked before anybody touches a mortar. Both used to be read by eye. This
+note is the contract for reading them with the tool.
+
+## the dry run log
+
+Every panel writes a log of the outputs it closed and when. `rehearse --log
+<csv>` reads that log against the compiled table.
+
+The file has a header row. The pin column is called `pin`, `address`, `cue`
+or `output`, the same spellings the continuity walk takes. The time column is
+called `time`, `fired`, `at` or `fired at`, and its values are written the
+way a cue time is: `12.4`, `1:23.450`, `250ms`, `4m30s`. A row whose pin or
+time does not read is reported and skipped, so one odd row does not stop the
+rest of the log being graded.
+
+Log times sit on the panel's own clock, which starts at the panel's first
+ignition. A show with a pre roll fires its opener before the show clock's
+zero, so every log time is the show time plus the pre roll, and the grade
+takes that off before comparing. A show compiled with `--absorb-preroll` has
+no pre roll left and the two clocks agree.
+
+Every show pin closes exactly once, so the earliest row for a pin is that
+pin's firing. Every other row is extra: a repeat row for a show pin, or any
+row for a pin the show never fires. A show pin with no row at all is missed.
+Each firing carries its offset from the table's time, positive when late.
+Past the tolerance, one frame of the show's format unless `--tolerance <ms>`
+says otherwise, a firing is late or early.
+
+Missed and extra outputs are errors, and so is a row that would not read;
+the command exits with the show problem code on any of them. Late and
+early outputs are warnings. The grade, the finding table
+and the summary line go to standard output, or to `--out`.
+
+## the walk after the show
+
+`continuity --after <csv>` reads the walk taken once the show is over. It
+takes the same file the pre-show walk takes.
+
+A match that fired has burnt its bridgewire and reads open. So a show pin
+that reads open fired, and one that still reads connected did not. With
+`--walk` also given, the pre-show walk splits the ones that did not fire: a
+pin that already read open before the show never had current and is dead,
+not misfired. Without it every connected show pin is a misfire. A show pin
+the after walk never reported is unknown and is treated as loaded, and a
+show pin reading short is listed as such and treated as loaded too. A pin
+outside the show that read connected before and open after is burnt: the
+panel fired something the table never held.
+
+Everything not fired gets the waiting period the misfire rules already give:
+half an hour for anything with a lift charge under it, five minutes for a
+ground piece. Every wait runs from the show's last light out, never from the
+cue or from the position's own last cue. Each position may be approached
+once the longest wait among its own unfired cues has run, and the field is
+clear when the last position is.
+
+A ground piece can be relit. Its refire goes onto a free pin at the same
+position, and after a show a free pin is one the after walk read open. A pin
+still reading connected has a lead on it, and a pin nobody walked is not
+known to be free. Shells are not refired; they are made safe.
+
+The command prints the clearance by position, the refire plan, the incident
+book and a summary line, and exits with the show problem code when anything
+did not fire or anything burnt.
+
+## worked example
+
+`examples/autumn/dryrun.csv` is a dry run log for the autumn show with one
+output skipped, three late and one extra. `examples/autumn/after.csv` is an
+after walk with two shells still in their tubes.
